\alias{TaskTorchUnsupervised}
\title{Generic Unsupervised Torch Task}
\description{
The unsupervised counterpart of \code{\link{TaskTorch}}: a general-purpose \code{\link[mlr3:Task]{Task}} without
target columns, for learning problems such as autoencoders, denoising or masked objectives and
contrastive pretraining.
It shares the task type \code{"torch"} with \code{\link{TaskTorch}}, so the same learners, pipeops and measures
work for both.

Because there are no target columns, there is nothing to infer the learning problem from, and
\code{output_dim} and \code{prediction_encoder} have to be given -- see section \emph{Inference} of
\code{\link{TaskTorch}} for what they do.
The batches of such a task have no \code{y} element by default, so the loss is called as
\code{loss(y_hat, NULL)} and has to ignore its second argument.
}
\section{Targets that are a Function of the Input}{

If the target of a batch is a function of its \emph{input} -- an autoencoder reconstructing its input,
a denoising or masked objective, contrastive pretraining -- pass a \code{target_batchgetter} that
declares an \code{x} argument, which receives the named list of feature tensors of the batch:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{as_task_torch(data, output_dim = ncol(data),
  target_batchgetter = function(data, x) x[[1L]],
  prediction_encoder = function(task, predict_tensor, predict_type) \{
    list(response = as.matrix(predict_tensor$cpu()))
  \})
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\section{Scoring}{
 \verb{$truth()} returns \code{NULL}, so measures of such a task read the ground truth
from the task itself, which \code{\link[=msr_torch]{msr_torch()}} arranges for any function that declares a \code{task}
argument.

A measure that is passed as \code{measure} becomes the task's default measure, which is what
\verb{$score()} and \verb{$aggregate()} use when they are called without arguments.
}

